2026-02-10 02:58:48,104 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Key Takeaways

- Derivatives are financial contracts set between two or more parties that derive their value from an underlying asset, a group of assets, or a benchmark.
- A derivative can trade on an exchange or over the counter (OTC).
- Prices for derivatives derive from fluctuations in the prices of underlying assets.
- Derivatives are usually leveraged instruments, which increases their potential risks and rewards.
- Common derivatives include futures contracts, forwards, options, and swaps. is relevant
2026-02-10 02:58:49,129 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 02:58:49,133 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document How Do Options Differ From Futures?

Both options and futures are types of derivative contracts that are based on some underlying asset or security. The main difference is that options contracts grant the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell the underlying in the future. Futures contracts, meanwhile, come with an obligation to buy or sell the underlying. is relevant
2026-02-10 02:58:50,042 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 02:58:50,046 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document What is an Underlying Asset

Underlying assets are the basic financial instruments or commodities that derivatives like options and futures are based on. The value of a derivative depends directly on the price or performance of its underlying asset, making these assets crucial in trading. Examples include stocks, bonds, commodities, or indexes. is relevant
2026-02-10 02:58:51,168 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 02:58:51,176 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Futures Trading: What It Is, How It Works, Factors, and Pros & Cons is relevant
2026-02-10 02:58:52,277 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 02:58:52,281 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document What Are the Main Benefits and Risks of Derivatives?

Derivatives can be a very convenient way to achieve financial goals. For example, a company that wants to hedge against its exposure to commodities can do so by buying or selling energy derivatives, such as crude oil futures. Similarly, a company could hedge its currency risk by purchasing currency-forward contracts. Derivatives can also help investors leverage their positions, such as by buying equities through stock options rather than shares.

The main drawbacks of derivatives include counterparty risk, the inherent risks of leverage, and the fact that complicated webs of derivative contracts can lead to systemic risks. is relevant
2026-02-10 02:58:53,216 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 02:58:53,219 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Exploring the Types of Options: Calls and Puts is relevant
